          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em sieve
            is a generalization of that of (right)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal+in+a+monoid"} ideal in a monoid
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %strong sieve on
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that are closed under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} precomposition
            with morphisms in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            Sometimes one says that a
            %strong sieve in
            a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            closed under precomposition with morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, def. 6.2.2.1
            ). If so, then a
            %em sieve on
            an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %em sieve in
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/X}
            %em sieve in
            a category is also naturally taken to be a collection of
            %em sieves on
            various objects. On the other hand, most authors speak just about
            %em sieves on
            an object anyway.
          %p
            %em Sieves on
            objects are an equivalent way to talk about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functors"} representable functors
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            category in terms of the total sets of
            %em elements
            of such a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subfunctor"} subfunctor
          %p#Attitude
            In this sense “sieve” is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concept+with+an+attitude"} concept with an attitude
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            as a
            %em sieve
            to indicate that one is interested in regarding it as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            of a (sifted)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            = succeed "." do
              %em covering sieves
          %p
            Accordingly, the classical examples of
            %em sieves on
            an object are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topologies"} Grothendieck topologies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            that behave like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverings"} coverings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            are actually
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            with respect to a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
          %p
            A choice of collections of morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \to c
            into an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
            for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in C
            reminds one of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(c) := \hom_C(-, c): C^{op} \to Set
            which assigns to each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in C
            the set of
            %em all
            morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is therefore for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in C
            a subset of the value of this functor evaluated at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            into this functor. Indeed, it turns out that one can assume without restriction of generality that the assignment of covers of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            can always be extended to such a monic natural transformation, and hence one formalizes the notion of “collection of covers” of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: F \hookrightarrow \hom(-, c)
            of the functor represented by
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %em sieve
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %p
            A dual notion is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosieve"} cosieve
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                sieve
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \hookrightarrow C
              that is both
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
              and a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete fibration
            %p
              A
              %em
                sieve on an object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              is a sieve in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/c
          %p Spelling this out, we arrive at the traditional definition of a sieve on an object:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em sieve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              on an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              is a subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset Ob(C/c)
              of the set of objects of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} over category
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              which is
              = succeed ":" do
                %em closed under precomposition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d \to c) \in S
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e \to d) \in Mor(C)
              then the composition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e \to d \to c)
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This is probably called a
              %em sieve
              because it “sifts out” the ‘special’ maps into
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              from the set of
              %em all
              maps into
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %p
              The French term for a sieve is
              = succeed "." do
                %em crible
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Sometimes the condition of a sieve being closed under the operation of precomposing with an arbitrary morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi e
                    %mo →
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: e \to d
              is called a “saturation condition”. Given any collection of morphisms targeted at
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %em saturate
              it, to obtain a sieve on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_fibrations Relation to fibrations
          %p
            Just as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
            is equivalent to a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to Cat
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete fibration
            is equivalent to a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is equivalent to a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the preordered category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+thinking"} negative thinking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega, Set, Cat
            are the categories of all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-categories"} (-1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-categories"} 0-categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-categories"} 1-categories
            respectively.
          %h3#relation_to_subfunctors Relation to subfunctors
          %p There is a canonical way to create subfunctors from sieves and sieves from subfunctors.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subfunctor"} subfunctor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functors"} representable functors
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Given a sieve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %strong subfunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi S
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_S \hookrightarrow Y(c)
              %strong defined by the sieve
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  that assigns to each object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in C
                  the set
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi d
                          %mo →
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_S(d) = \{(d \to c) \in S\}
              %li
                %p
                  that assigns to each morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi d
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d \to d') \in C
                  the function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_S(d') \to F_S(d)
                  induced on elements by precomposition with
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo →
                          %mi d
                          %mo ′
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \to d'
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Given a subfunctor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \hookrightarrow Y(c)
              %strong sieve defined by the subfunctor
              is given by
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi F
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi d
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Mor
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_F \coloneqq \{g: d \to c \in Mor(C) | (Y(d) \stackrel{Y(g)}\to Y(c)) = (Y(d)  \to F \to Y(c)) \}
            %p or equivalently
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi Obj
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_F = \coprod_{d \in Obj(C)}F(d)
                \.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              These two definitions establish a bijection between sieves on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              and subobjects of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(c)
            %p
              For every sieve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    S_{F_S} = S
            %p and for every subfunctor we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_{S_F} = F
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remarks Remarks
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The construction of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_F
                  makes sense for every morphism of presheaves
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo →
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to Y(c)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                  of this map,
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi F
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mi im
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi F
                            %mo →
                            %mi Y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        S_{F} = S_{im (F \to Y(c))}
                        \\,.
              %li
                %p
                  In the presence of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
                  a morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to Y(c)
                  is sometimes called a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+epimorphism"} local epimorphism
                  if the sieve
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_F
                  is a covering sieve. If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to Y(c)
                  is actually a subfunctor, then it is called a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+monomorphism"} dense monomorphism
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  of a subfunctor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi S
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: F_S \hookrightarrow Y(c) = \hom(-, c)
                  along any morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(-, g): \hom(-, d) \to \hom(-, c)
                  is again a subfunctor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mo *
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^* F
                  of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mo *
                        %mi S
                        %mo =
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                          %mrow
                            %mi e
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi Ob
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi e
                        %mo →
                        %mi d
                        %mo :
                        %mi g
                        %mi f
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        g^* S = \bigcup_{e \in Ob(C)} \{f: e \to d: g f \in S\}
                        \\,.
              %li
                %p
                  A sieve
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_F
                  on
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi F
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: F \hookrightarrow \hom(-, c)
                  a subfunctor, may be described as a function which assigns to each object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                  a collection of morphisms
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: d \to c
                  into
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  means that whenever
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: d \to c
                  belongs to the sieve and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi e
                        %mo →
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: e \to d
                  is any morphism, then
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi e
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f g: e \to c
                  also belongs to the sieve.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              The subfunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi S
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_S \hookrightarrow X
              corresponding to a sieve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coimage"} coimage
              of the morphism out of the disjoint union of all objects (regarded as representable presheaves) in the sieve:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi α
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (U = \coprod_{(U_\alpha \to X) \in S} U_\alpha)  \to X
            %p in that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi colim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (F_S \to X) = ((colim(U \times_X U \stackrel{\to}{\to} U)   \to X)
                    \\,.
          %p
            If the sieve is generated by (is the saturation of) a collection of morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi α
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_\alpha \to U\}
            then the same statement remains true with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            being the coproduct over just these
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              As described at
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+and+colimits+by+example"} limits and colimits by example
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
              tells us that for each object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi colim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %msub
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                            %mi U
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo →
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %mi colim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi U
                            %msub
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo →
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %mi colim
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mo ×
                              %mrow
                                %mi Hom
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi V
                                %mo ,
                                %mi X
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo →
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    colim(U \times_X U \stackrel{\to}{\to} U)(V)
                    \&amp; \simeq
                    colim(Hom(V,U \times_X U) \stackrel{\to}{\to} Hom(V,U))
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \simeq
                    colim( Hom(V,U) \times_{Hom(V,X)} Hom(V,U)  \stackrel{\to}{\to} Hom(V,U))
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              where all objects appearing (at least in the first lines) are implicitly regarded as presheaves under the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %p But this colimit now manifestly computes the set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mtext maps from
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mtext to
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mtext that factor through
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{\text{maps from }\,V\,\text{ to }\,X\,\text{ that factor through }\,U\}_\sim
            %p where the equivalence relation is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mtext and
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mtext coincide as maps to
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ((f \to U \to X) \sim (g \to U \to X))
                    \\Leftrightarrow
                    (f\,\text{ and }\,g\,\text{ coincide as maps to }\,X)
                    \\,.
            %p
              So the set is just the set of maps from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              that factor through one of the
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_S(V)
              assigned by the subfunctor corresponding to the sieve.
          %h3#sieves_as_covers Sieves as covers
          %h4#introduction_and_overview Introduction and overview
          %p The following is a pedagogical step-by-step description of the crucial aspects of sieves as covers.
          %p
            To start with the simplest example that already contains in it all the relevant aspects, consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with an open subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset X
            that is covered by two open subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \subset X
            in that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cup U_2
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            coincides with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U_1 \cup U_2 = V
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+coproducts"} disjoint coproducts
            that we are used to from categories like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            (a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %em does
            have disjoint coproducts.
          %p
            Another way to think of this is obtained by first forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cap U_2
            sitting in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  U_1 \times_V U_2 &amp;\to&amp; U_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  U_2 &amp;\to&amp; V
                  }
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
          %p
            The union
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cup U_2
            is again obtained from this by removing in the above diagram the bottom right corner and then forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            over the resulting diagram: this is again
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∪
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo =
                          %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  U_1 \times_V U_2 &amp;\to&amp; U_1
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  U_2 &amp;\to&amp; U_1 \cup U_2 = V
                  }
          %p
            is not only a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            also a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            diagram.
          %p
            The important point about (covering) sieves is that they show up when the above situation is sent via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
              preserves
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          %p
            As a result, the above discussion goes through equivalently for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
            by our open subsets all the way up to the last pushout. In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            that last pushout reproduced the open subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(X) = [Op(X)^{op}, Set]
            it instead reproduces the
            %em sieve
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
          %p
            Let’s go through this in detail. First of all notice that in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(X)
            all limits and colimits do exist (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+and+colimits+by+example"} limits and colimits by example
            for more on that); in particular the coproduct
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⨿
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2)
          %p
            exists. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            which we here indicate explicitly, even though often and elsewhere, notably elsewhere in this entry here, it is notationally suppressed.
          %p
            For the following it is helpful to say explicitly what the presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⨿
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2)
            is like. Since, as described at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+and+colimits+by+example"} limits and colimits by example
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subset X
            yields the set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⨿
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⨿
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⨿
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  (Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2))(W)
                  \&amp;=
                  Y(U_1)(W) \amalg Y(U_2)(W)
                  \\\
                  \&amp;=
                  Hom(W,U_1) \amalg Hom(W, U_2)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            where the coproducts on the right are just those in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            which are just ordinary disjoint unions of sets.
          %p
            So this says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⨿
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2)
            is the presheaf that assigns to any open set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            the disjoint union of the collections of maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            and those from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            there is either none or one such map in each case, but it is helpful to speak generally of “sets of all maps”, since that is the general intuition useful for presheaf categories.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
            just happens to be a particularly simple example.)
          %p
            Notice that in particular a given map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to V
            which factors both through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \to V
            as well as through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2 \to V
            will appear as two distinct elements in the set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⨿
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2))(W)
          %p
            But first consider the fiber product from before, now after having applied the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(U_1) \times_{Y(V)} Y(U_2)
            in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mrow
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi V
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y(U_1) \times_{Y(V)} Y(U_2) &amp;\to&amp; Y(U_2)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y(U_1)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Y(V)
                  }
          %p
            is the same as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi V
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(U_1 \times_V U_2) = Y(U_1 \cap U_2)
          %p
            But this is also easily checked explicitly. We go through this because this kind of reasoning for computing limits and colimits of presheaves will be needed throughout here: since for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            the covariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Psh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Psh
                  %mo →
                  %mi PSh
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Psh(Y(W),-) : Psh \to PSh
            preserves limits (by the very definition of
            = succeed "!)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            a pullback diagram of sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mrow
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi V
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Hom(Y(W),Y(U_1) \times_{Y(V)} Y(U_2))
                  \&amp;\to&amp; Hom(Y(W),Y(U_2))
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Hom(Y(W),Y(U_1))
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Hom(Y(W),Y(V))
                  }
          %p
            Again by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            this is simply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mrow
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi V
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  (Y(U_1) \times_{Y(V)} Y(U_2) )(W)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Hom(W,U_2)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Hom(W,U_1)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Hom(W,V)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p This being a pullback diagram now says in words:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y(U_1) \times_{Y(V)} Y(U_2) )(W)
              is the set of those pairs of maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_2
              that coincide as maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_1 \to V
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_2 \to V
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Clearly, this set is the same as the set of maps into the intersection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cap U_2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Y(U_1) \times_{Y(V)} Y(U_2) = Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)
                  \= Y(U_1 \cap U_2)
                  \\,.
          %p So far so long-winded. Now let’s see what happens when we now form the pushout over
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y(U_1 \times_V U_2) &amp;\to&amp; Y(U_2)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y(U_1)
                  }
          %p
            that will go, for a moment, by its canonical but lengthy name
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi V
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Y(U_1) \coprod_{Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)} Y(U_2)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                            %mrow
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mo ×
                                %mi V
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y(U_1 \times_V U_2) &amp;\to&amp; Y(U_2)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y(U_1)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Y(U_1) \coprod_{Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)} Y(U_2)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Again, we can figure out what this presheaf is by computing objectwise what it does to any open subset
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Hom
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                            %mrow
                              %mi Y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mo ×
                                %mi V
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Hom(W, U_1 \times_V U_2)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  Hom(W, U_2)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Hom(W,U_1)
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  (Y(U_1) \coprod_{Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)} Y(U_2))(W)
                  }
          %p
            must be a colimit in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %blockquote
            %p
              The set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mo ×
                          %mi V
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y(U_1) \coprod_{Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)} Y(U_2))(W)
              is the quotient of the disjoint union of the collection of maps from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              into
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
              and those from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              into
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_2
              if they both factor through a map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_1 \times_X U_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \cap U_2
              and coincide there.
          %p
            But this just means that contrary to the plain coproduct
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⨿
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to U_2
            that coincide as maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to X
            are no longer regarded as different elements of our set given by the pushout presheaf, but are regarded as being the same.
          %p So this means we find that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi V
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mtext maps
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mtext that factor through either
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mtext or
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (Y(U_1) \coprod_{Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)} Y(U_2))(W)
                  \=
                  \\{
                  \\text{ maps } \,W \to V \,\text{ that factor through either }\, U_1
                  \\,\text{ or }\, U_2
                  \\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            But this is by definition the assignment of the subfunctor corresponding to the sieve on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \to V
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2 \to V
          %p So we find that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi sieve
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi V
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_{sieve(U_1,U_2)}
                  \=
                  Y(U_1) \coprod_{Y(U_1 \times_V U_2)} Y(U_2)
                  \\,.
          %p Given that we made it to this point, we should go one small step further that will be very useful.
          %p
            In the present simple example we worked with a cover given by just two objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2
          %p
            Namely, let’s come back to the observation that the coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⨿
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y(U_1) \amalg Y(U_2)
            does exist. Let’s just call this presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{U}
            (not in general a
            = succeed "!)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
          %p
            Then it is easy to see by the same kind of objectwise reasoning that the colimiting presheaf that we are after is equivalently the colimit over the pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+morphisms"} parallel morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{U} \times_{Y(V)} \mathbf{U}
                  \\stackrel{\stackrel{p_2}{\to}}{\stackrel{p_1}{\to}}
                  \\mathbf{U}
          %p in that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi sieve
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi colim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_{sieve(U_1,U_2)} \simeq
                  colim (
                  \\mathbf{U} \times_{Y(V)} \mathbf{U}
                  \\stackrel{\stackrel{p_2}{\to}}{\stackrel{p_1}{\to}}
                  \\mathbf{U}
                  )
                  \\,.
          %p
            This description now has an evident direct generalization to the case where instead of just
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \to V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_2 \to V
            we have an arbitrary collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to V\}
            of open sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            covering
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi i
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{U} := \coprod_i Y(U(i))
          %p that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi sieve
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi colim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_{sieve(\{U_i\})} \simeq
                  colim (
                  \\mathbf{U} \times_{Y(V)} \mathbf{U}
                  \\stackrel{\stackrel{p_2}{\to}}{\stackrel{p_1}{\to}}
                  \\mathbf{U}
                  )
          %p
            is the presheaf that to every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            assigns the set of all maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to V
            that factor through any one of the
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
          %p
            It is in this way that sieves and their associated subfunctors encapsulate the notion of
            %em cover
            of an object
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            do factor through the cover.
          %p And, to end this pedagogical piece with an outlook to indicate the gain in understanding this achieves:
          %p
            once we start forming
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{U} \times_{Y(V)} \mathbf{U} \stackrel{\to}{\to} \mathbf{U}
            there is no stopping. We can keep forming higher and higher such fiber products
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
